    {
      "text": "###In-Memory Persistence and Memory Management\n\n-  Spark’s performance advantage over MapReduce is greatest in use cases involving repeated\ncomputations. Much of this performance increase is due to Spark’s use of in-memory persistence.\nRather than writing to disk between each pass through the data, **Spark has the option of keeping the\ndata on the executors loaded into memory.** That way, the data on each partition is available inmemory\neach time it needs to be accessed\n\n  1. In memory as deserialized Java objects\n    - store objects in RDDs is as the original deserialized Java objects that\nare defined by the driver program. This form of in-memory storage is the fastest, since it reduces\nserialization time; however, it may not be the most memory efficient, since it requires the data to\nbe stored as objects.\n\n  2. As serialized data\n     - Using the standard Java serialization library, Spark objects are converted into streams of\nbytes as they are moved around the network. This approach may be slower, since serialized\ndata is more CPU-intensive to read than deserialized data; however, it is often more\nmemory efficient, since it allows the user to choose a more efficient representation. While\nJava serialization is more efficient than full objects, Kryo serialization\n\n  3. on disk\n    - RDDs, whose partitions are too large to be stored in RAM on each of the executors, can be\nwritten to disk. This strategy is obviously slower for repeated computations\n\n`**NOTE**`\n```\nThe persist() function in the RDD class lets the user control how the RDD is stored. By default,\npersist() stores an RDD as deserialized objects in memory```",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "0a35bf4490ea409383bfe"
    },
    {
      "text": "####Types of RDDs\n- The implementation of the Spark Scala API contains an abstract class, RDD, which contains not only\nthe five core functions of RDDs, but also those transformations and actions that are available to all\nRDDs,\n\n`*NOTE*`\n```\nFind out what type an RDD is by using the toDebugString function, which is defined on all RDDs. This will tell you what\nkind of RDD you have and provide a list of its parent RDDs.\n```",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "06370c8aaaed4e9982cca"
    },
